                Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang delivers his keynote on the company&apos;s GTC event in Berlin, Germany, Wednesday, Oct. 21, 2026.
                
                    
                    Markus Schreiber/AP
                    
                
                hide caption
            
        

            
              
              toggle caption
                i

            
    

    
        
        Markus Schreiber/AP
        
    

   
   

The massive artificial intelligence chip maker Nvidia says it is launching an internal investigation, after reports that the company&apos;s CEO was in a close relationship with a Chinese spy.   

In the statement announcing the investigation, Nvidia cited recent reporting by The New York Times, which alleges Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang was the target of a Chinese intelligence operation that sought to send advanced Nvidia AI chips to China.   

&quot;While the Board has no reason to believe that either national security or NVIDIA secrets were compromised, the Board&apos;s inquiry will examine all available information,&quot; the statement from the company&apos;s board of directors reads in part.   
   

The Times released a series of reports over the last two weeks detailing Huang&apos;s alleged relationship with a woman named Alice Huang, who is accused of being a spy for China. The two are not related. NPR has reached out to the Chinese Embassy in Washington, D.C. but has not received a response.   

