                New Zealand Prime Minister and National Party leader, Christopher Luxon addresses party faithful in Auckland, New Zealand, Saturday, Nov.7, 2026.
                
                    
                    Alex Robertson/AP
                    
                
                hide caption
            
        

            
              
              toggle caption
                i

            
    

    
        
        Alex Robertson/AP
        
    

   
   

WELLINGTON, New Zealand — New Zealanders will wait for weeks to discover who will lead the country after Saturday&apos;s election failed to deliver a decisive win for the right or the left, leaving fraught negotiations for a wafer-thin governing majority the only apparent path to victory for either side.   

The tightest vote in years delivered a stinging rebuke to the major parties which have long dominated New Zealand politics, Christopher Luxon&apos;s center-right National party and center-left Labour led by Chris Hipkins, as voters threw more support behind minor parties on the ideological flanks than before. On Saturday&apos;s numbers, minor parties would enter negotiations for a governing deal with leverage to extract deeper policy concessions from a weakened National or Labour.   
   
            
                  

         
                        

