FLORIDA, Uruguay — Pope Leo XIV wrapped up his visit to Uruguay on Sunday by praising the women who have passed the Catholic faith onto younger generations, as he highlighted the role the church has played since the founding of Latin America&apos;s most secular country.   

Leo traveled to the city of Florida, north of Montevideo, to mark an important Catholic feast day before heading to Argentina for the second leg of his South American tour. It&apos;s expected to be the most politically delicate stop, given the Catholic Church&apos;s sharp criticisms of the austerity program of libertarian President Javier Milei.   
   
            
                  

         
                        

